Before: one class owns pricing, saving, and formatting.

```java
class Invoice {
    double calculateTotal(List<LineItem> items) {
        return items.stream().mapToDouble(i -> i.price * i.qty).sum();
    }
    void saveToDatabase(Connection conn) {
        conn.execute("INSERT INTO invoices ...");
    }
    String formatAsPdfText() {
        return "INVOICE\nTotal: " + calculateTotal(items);
    }
}
```

After: each responsibility gets its own class, and `Invoice` becomes a plain data holder.

```java
class InvoicePricer {
    double calculateTotal(List<LineItem> items) { /* pricing rules */ }
}
class InvoiceRepository {
    void save(Invoice invoice, Connection conn) { /* persistence */ }
}
class InvoicePdfFormatter {
    String format(Invoice invoice) { /* layout */ }
}
```

A tax-rate change now only touches `InvoicePricer` — the database and PDF code are untouched and can't be accidentally broken by that edit.
